Subject: Cantor Shared Inference Communication Assessment

Description: A non-authoritative research assessment of outside intelligences negotiating a reasoning walk through language, shared state, learned messages, hidden representations, or internalized agent perspectives during inference.

& [AssessmentIdentity] is the bounded research record
  + [assessment_uuid] is 21adf89b-03c5-47b6-a0d3-836c9796eb04
  + [captured_at] is 2026-07-29T10:03:12.4677434-05:00
  + [status] is current_primary_source_landscape
  + [implementation_authority] is absent
  = conclusion: cooperative reasoning with outside model intelligence is established research, including direct latent communication, while Cantor's signed semantic negotiation and proof boundary remains a distinct unproven composition

& [InferenceMeaningDelineation] is the three different meanings of during inference
  + [job_level_inference] is one problem-solving session containing multiple model calls, tool calls, messages, checkpoints, and revisions
  + [generation_level_inference] is one autoregressive generation that may stop or yield at tool and communication boundaries before a later continuation call
  + [forward_pass_level_inference] is one model invocation whose hidden representations are directly exchanged, injected, transformed, or jointly computed with another model
  = must: state which meaning is intended before comparing systems
  = conclusion: most production multi-agent frameworks implement job-level cooperation; latent-communication research reaches model representations but does not make independent models one ordinary shared transformer forward pass

& [ResearchLineage] is the material prior-art progression
  + [blackboard_systems] is cooperating independent knowledge sources posting hypotheses and evidence into a global working structure, established by Hearsay-II
  + [differentiable_agent_communication] is learned real-valued inter-agent messages and end-to-end training through a communication channel, demonstrated by DIAL in multi-agent reinforcement learning
  + [LLM_multiagent_debate] is multiple model instances proposing, criticizing, and revising answers across inference rounds
  + [conversational_agent_frameworks] is configurable LLM, human, and tool participants communicating through orchestrated messages, exemplified by AutoGen
  + [layered_model_aggregation] is later model calls receiving outputs from multiple earlier models as auxiliary context, exemplified by Mixture-of-Agents
  + [LLM_blackboard_systems] is role-selected LLM agents repeatedly reading and updating shared problem state until bounded consensus
  + [latent_interagent_communication] is direct transmission of continuous last hidden states between LLM agents, including learned compression and heterogeneous-model experiments, demonstrated by Interlat
  + [internalized_multiagent_debate] is distillation of explicit agent debate into one model with agent-associated activation subspaces
  + [latent_channel_security] is recognition that shared hidden states and KV caches may leak private context and require representation-level safeguards

& [EvidenceBoundary] is what the literature currently supports
  + [supported] is models can improve one another through repeated attributed textual exchange on some tasks
  + [supported] is neural agents can learn continuous communication protocols under shared training objectives
  + [supported] is LLM hidden states can be transmitted through learned inter-agent latent interfaces in bounded evaluated systems
  + [supported] is explicit debate can be compressed or internalized by post-training
  + [mixed] is multi-agent debate does not reliably beat strong compute-matched single-agent baselines across tasks
  + [open] is whether latent inter-agent exchange improves general semantic negotiation, truthfulness, safety, or long-horizon coordination outside benchmark distributions
  + [open] is how to preserve inspectable meaning, provenance, authority, disagreement, and security when communication bypasses language
  - never: infer correctness, shared understanding, or semantic agreement merely from communication bandwidth or consensus

& [CantorComparison] is the overlap and remaining hypothesis
  + [established_overlap] is external shared state, addressed participants, iterative proposals and challenges, model heterogeneity, bounded rounds, and optional learned channels
  + [Cantor_specific_hypothesis] is agents negotiate typed semantic moves against one signed SOP anchor fabric while Cantor verifies identity, authority, evidence, contradiction, state version, and proof at every committed step
  + [difference] is Cantor treats the shared structure as an authoritative semantic blackboard and transaction log rather than only a transcript, scratchpad, or latent message channel
  + [research_extension] is a latent channel may later propose or compress non-authoritative semantic moves while a structured shadow channel preserves the inspectable claim, source, uncertainty, and gate decision
  = conclusion: Cantor should not claim invention of shared inference or latent communication; its testable contribution is governed semantic negotiation around those mechanisms

& [RecommendedArchitecture] is the correct first manifestation
  + [authoritative_channel] is typed attributed SemanticExchangePacket records over the signed Cantor junction
  + [participant_execution] is independent model calls between explicit semantic rendezvous barriers
  + [walk_protocol] is observe, propose move, resolve address, challenge, gate, commit or reject, project the new state, and continue
  + [shared_state] is immutable anchor bundle plus append-only proposition, evidence, disagreement, and decision graph
  + [optional_latent_channel] is a later model-pair-specific hidden-state, embedding, or KV transport used only for proposals or compression
  + [shadow_projection] is every consequential latent contribution projected into a typed inspectable proposition before admission
  + [authority_rule] is no latent message directly authorizes an SOP meaning, instruction, permission, or effect
  = rationale: checkpointed semantic exchange is provider-independent, replayable, debuggable, and suitable for collecting training data for a later latent junction

& [MinimumComparativeProof] is the test before model-internal coupling
  + [condition_A] is one model with self-review under a fixed compute budget
  + [condition_B] is two identical models exchanging text packets
  + [condition_C] is two heterogeneous models exchanging the same packet schema
  + [condition_D] is condition C through a shared Cantor semantic blackboard and deterministic gates
  + [condition_E] is condition D plus a model-specific latent proposal channel
  + [measures] is semantic-move accuracy, material objection yield, false consensus, source fidelity, unresolved-difference preservation, latency, tokens, privacy leakage, replayability, and marginal value per added pass
  = must: require D to establish value before E is attempted
  = must: test adversarial latent leakage and semantic shadow-projection loss before E can affect consequential work

